

We design the experimental protocol around three properties the methodology of Section~\ref{sec:methodology} is meant to deliver: (i)~accurate classification of known preliminary actions (PAs), (ii)~reliable identification of \emph{unseen} PAs as \texttt{unknown} rather than as the nearest known class, and (iii)~response selection that converts those verdicts into measurable QR-CWoS. Each of these properties is evaluated in isolation and in combination, so that we can attribute end-to-end window quality to specific components of the pipeline.

\subsection{Research Questions}
\label{subsec:rqs}

The experiments are organized around four questions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ1.} Does the varMax open-set rule of Section~\ref{subsec:varmax} improve detection of unseen PAs relative to closed-set softmax and to alternative open-set baselines, on RF I/Q data captured over the air?
    \item \textbf{RQ2.} Does the DQN response policy of Section~\ref{subsec:dqn} produce longer and more reliable QR-CWoS windows than rule-based or threshold-based response policies operating on the same classifier output?
    \item \textbf{RQ3.} How does end-to-end QR-CWoS quality degrade as the prevalence of unseen tactics in the test stream increases, and is degradation graceful (a slow decline in window duration with novelty flags raised) or catastrophic (silent misclassification with no window opened)?
    \item \textbf{RQ4.} How sensitive are the open-set verdict and the resulting window duration to the varMax threshold $\tau$ and to the weighting of the duration, resource, and miss terms in the DQN reward?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Dataset}
\label{subsec:dataset}

We use the PA dataset constructed by the OTA pipeline of~\cite{trott2026model}. The dataset is built in two stages. \TODO{This is outdated. New data is being prepared.}

\paragraph{Digital pilot stage.} Each of the four trained PA classes (PA2 Burst, PA3 Sustain, PA4 Hop, PA8 Replay) is first generated synthetically with controlled parameters (burst lengths, dwell times, hop sets, replay-segment durations) and validated by the digital validators developed in MS1--MS2 of the parent project. The digital stage establishes that the classifier and event-detection front end can recover each PA's defining behavior under ideal channel conditions, and provides the parameter sweeps from which OTA emission settings are drawn.

\paragraph{Over-the-air stage.} Validated digital pilots are transmitted through a software-defined radio testbed and recaptured at a separate SDR receiver, with TX/RX synchronization and on-site validation as described in Trott's MS3 protocol~\cite{trott2026model}. OTA recordings are segmented into fixed-length analysis windows of \TODO{report window length in ms and sample rate}, yielding the $x \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times T \times F}$ tensors of Section~\ref{subsec:preprocessing}. Only OTA windows that pass the synchronization and payload-integrity validators are retained; the rejection rate is reported in the results.

\paragraph{Train/validation/test split.} OTA samples are partitioned into a training set (\TODO{X\%}), a validation set (\TODO{Y\%}) used for varMax threshold calibration and DQN early stopping, and a held-out test set (\TODO{Z\%}). To prevent leakage from temporally adjacent windows, splitting is performed at the recording-session level rather than at the window level: all windows from a given OTA capture session belong to the same split.

\paragraph{Unseen-tactic protocol.} For each experimental run we designate one of the four PA classes as the \emph{held-out unseen tactic}. The held-out class contributes \emph{no} samples to training or validation; its OTA windows appear only in the test set, where the pipeline must label them \texttt{unknown}. We rotate the held-out class across runs (PA2-out, PA3-out, PA4-out, PA8-out) so that no single class drives the reported numbers, and we report per-class as well as aggregate results. Where applicable we also evaluate a \emph{multi-unseen} variant in which two PAs are held out simultaneously, to stress-test the open-set boundary.

